[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Which storage approach should the design use? → A: Versioned JSON blob (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now the design. Two sections — the module itself, then how the app and tests consume it.

  [1mSection 1: The preferences module[0m

  [1mFile:[0m [38;5;153mpreferences.js[39m at the repo root, alongside [38;5;153mapp.js[39m. ([38;5;153msrc/[39m is the unrelated CommonJS demo code; this feature never
  touches it.)

  [1mStorage key:[0m [38;5;153mwebapp.prefs.v1[39m — a single JSON object. The [38;5;153mv1[39m is the migration seam: a future shape change writes [38;5;153mv2[39m and
  reads [38;5;153mv1[39m once to convert.

  [1mInterface:[0m

  [33mcreatePreferences[39m({ storage })   [32m// factory; storage defaults to localStorage[39m
    .[33mget[39m(key)                      [32m// stored value, else the default, else undefined[39m
    .[33mgetAll[39m()                      [32m// defaults merged with stored values[39m
    .[33mset[39m(key, value)               [32m// persist, notify subscribers[39m
    .[33mclear[39m()                       [32m// remove the key entirely, back to defaults[39m
    .[33msubscribe[39m(listener)           [32m// returns an unsubscribe function[39m

  Plus a default instance bound to real [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, which is what [38;5;153mapp.js[39m imports. The factory exists so tests can
  inject a fake storage object — that's what makes this testable under [38;5;153mnode --test[39m with no jsdom and no dependencies.

  [1mDefaults:[0m a frozen [38;5;153mDEFAULTS[39m object owned by the module. [38;5;153mget[39m falls back to it, so callers never branch on [38;5;153mundefined[39m or
  duplicate a default. Starts as [38;5;153m{ rememberedUsername: "" }[39m.

